\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:future-work}

This work develops a three-dimensional quaternion MEKF in which a latent
Ornstein--Uhlenbeck acceleration prior drives velocity, oscillatory displacement,
and an integral-displacement state whose zero pseudo-measurement provides
low-frequency drift control.  The Live stability analysis follows the source
modes directly.  While accelerometer-bias learning is held, the active 18-state
subsystem is uniformly exponentially stable under the stated translational and
finite-window attitude/gyro-bias information conditions, with the frozen bias
entering as a bounded input.  After bias release, the deployed finite
correlation time of the residual accelerometer-bias state contracts any
unobserved residual-bias direction in the active 21-state recursion.  The
positive OU process floor and exact OU-driven integration chain provide active
translational process excitation analytically.  Separated accepted
specific-force--magnetic vector packets provide an explicit sufficient
attitude/gyro-bias information margin, and the bounded adaptation envelope shows
that the particular tuning exponents and EMA horizons are performance choices
rather than stability-critical feedback parameters.  Without an absolute
heading reference, the same estimator structure descends to the yaw quotient
rather than assigning convergence to absolute yaw.

The quantitative deployed certificate is source reachable.  Separate
fixed-dimensional graphs are used for the held 18-state and active 21-state
normal-Live modes, and source-complete words carry path-dependent quadratic
metrics for the additive coordinates.  Attitude is not reduced to a local
Euclidean radius: the certificate uses
$V_R=1-\cos\theta$ and an exact Rodrigues identity for the energy change under
the implemented finite quaternion correction.  The complete accepted Kalman
innovation, including the full $S\to\theta$ cross-covariance path, remains in
that identity.  A rigorous source-word dissipation bound can therefore certify
any geodesic chart $\theta\le\theta_*<\pi$ on which the exact correction remains
dissipative after accounting for coupled non-attitude errors.  The antipodal
set and genuine measurement critical sets remain excluded, so no global MEKF
convergence claim is made.

Startup is quantitative.  For the measurement-only Mahony gravity-direction
observer with integral gyro-bias feedback, Sec.~\ref{sec:semiglobal-proxy}
derives an explicit practical-ISS rate, input gain, tilt envelope, and
observable-bias envelope on a compact subset of the almost-global basin.  The
implemented accelerometer reset discards the stored pre-reset attitude.  Under
$\|a_f\|<g$ its image lies in the desired gravity hemisphere for every prior
stored attitude, so source-level entry is global with respect to that discarded
prior state while the subsequent continuous observer remains properly
almost-global/regional.  The accepted compact handoff set $\mathcal H$ is then
embedded directly in source-node Lyapunov funnels.  Source-shaped handoff
reachability is certified by propagating scalar funnel levels over exact
source-reachable words and jumps rather than recursively enclosing the full
18--21 dimensional reachable set or propagating coordinate-radius boxes.
Transient one-sample growth is allowed when the complete certified word
contracts and every prefix remains proof safe.

A timeout with a heading gauge enters the same full-heading funnel chain.  An
ungauged timeout instead receives a regional practical-ISS result on the yaw
quotient; later magnetic acquisition selects a horizontal representative and
enters the full-heading chain only when its exact jump image is certified.
Magnetic gauge changes and Live tilt re-locks are explicit hybrid jumps.  The
jump-followed-by-flow result of Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery} is
expressed in the same source-node metric: the tilt reset has a reset-to-funnel
bound in which discarded pre-reset tilt does not enter the multiplicative loop
gain, and the mandatory cooldown is bounded by products of certified source-word
contractions.  Continuous hard-iron correction writes no attitude or covariance
and remains part of the bounded normal-Live measurement schedule.

Bounded departures from the OU latent-acceleration model enter those same
source-word inequalities as deterministic disturbances.  Non-OU harmonics,
hydrodynamic forcing, and bounded correlation-time error therefore enlarge the
ultimate funnel level rather than invalidate the stability argument.  The
stochastic qualification also uses the same metric.  A product-manifold distance
combining $\SO(3)$ geodesic separation and additive-state separation bounds the
localized noisy-to-zero-noise word endpoint; conditional second/fourth-moment
bounds then give a constructive regional mean-square drift inequality without
assuming that NIS gating preserves zero conditional mean.  Because Gaussian
noise has unbounded support, compact-funnel almost-sure invariance is not
claimed; the exact Gaussian process receives a finite-horizon high-probability
qualification through the localization-exit bound.

The adaptation layer separates sea time scale, OU-prior amplitude, and integral
regularization.  The measured sea period sets $\tau$, the physical band-limited
acceleration RMS is mapped to $\sigma_{aw}$, and the $S=0$ pseudo-measurement is
set independently by the reduced physical-MSE balance
\begin{equation}
r_S^\star\propto
q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}m_{-4}^{3/7}T_S^{-1/2}.
\end{equation}
For a fixed-shape sea with
$m_{-4}\propto\sigma_a^2\tau^8$, strong-observation
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ independent of sea amplitude to leading order, and
$T_S\propto\tau$, this becomes
\begin{equation}
\boxed{r_S^\star\propto\sigma_a^{6/7}\tau^{41/14}.}
\end{equation}
The exact spectral evaluation reproduces these exponents over the reference
spectra and gives the deployed coefficient $C_J\simeq0.0538$.

Under the paired ten-seed stationary JONSWAP protocol, normalized vertical RMS
displacement error is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$ for OU--III versus
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II, with a paired difference of
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage points and bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$.
PM--Stokes gives the same qualitative vertical result.  Mean 3-D displacement
RMS is also lower for the current isotropic OU--III configuration in each
stationary JONSWAP sea and in the controlled transition, with paired bootstrap
intervals excluding zero.  These simulation results apply to the tested
directional and spectral families, not to absolute navigation.

The horizontal analysis explains why isotropic regularization can remain useful
despite much larger low-frequency horizontal acceleration error.  The scalar
spectral reduction identifies how posterior acceleration-error intensity,
pseudo-measurement covariance, and cadence jointly place the low-frequency
correction corner, but it does not determine universal world-axis gains in the
presence of attitude coupling and heading-dependent orbital motion.  Reducing
tilt error, improving accelerometer-bias observability, or adding horizontal
aiding remains a more direct route to reducing the horizontal disturbance.  The
observed $x/y$ split is heading dependent and does not support universal
world-axis tuning constants.

The remaining analytical gap in the adaptation study is the complete 21-state
physical error $H_2$ problem.  The reduced performance model does not retain
attitude/gravity leakage, gyroscope and accelerometer bias paths, OU-model
mismatch, cross-covariance coupling, or the full periodic update sequence.
Section~\ref{sec:adaptation-full-state-h2} formulates that physical displacement
objective and provides the natural next step for assessing how those couplings
modify the reduced SpectralMSE exponents and coefficient.  This performance gap
is distinct from the stability certificate, which already treats bounded
OU-model mismatch as a disturbance.

The principal limitations remain the primarily simulation-based evidence,
comparison centered on OU--II, controlled rather than general changing-sea
cases, fixed-temperature study, lack of physical off-CoG lever-arm validation,
and incomplete magnetic-distortion model.  The startup and timeout bounds remain
conditional on declared physical disturbance, seed, heading, and non-attitude
state bounds.  The widened stability theory is not yet a numerically
instantiated deployed certificate: the continuous source graph and word
families, path metrics $P_i$, group weights $a_{R,i}$, largest verified
$\theta_{*,i}$, exact source-word margin $\mu_W$, handoff funnel levels and
transition inequalities, hybrid cooldown/reset constants, and stochastic
product-distance/noise constants must still be enclosed rigorously.  The
ESP32--S3 exercise establishes source portability but not processor, memory,
power, or deadline margins.  Reported displacement remains bounded wave motion
about a local reference; no global navigation position is claimed without
external aiding.

\subsection*{Future work}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.25em]
  \item \textbf{Full-state physical $H_2$ analysis.}  Solve the periodic
        21-state Riccati and physical-error recursions of
        Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-full-state-h2} at the calibrated sea states and
        compare their predicted minimizing amplitudes and local exponents with
        the deployed SpectralMSE $6/7,41/14$ law and complete-estimator behavior.
  \item \textbf{Source-funnel certificate instantiation.}  Rigorously enclose
        the deployed source graph and source-complete word families, solve and
        verify the path metrics, determine the largest admissible large-angle
        sectors, bound $\mu_W$, initialize the handoff funnel directly from
        $\mathcal H$, and evaluate hybrid and stochastic margins.  Report
        diagnostic coordinate radii only after the funnel certificate itself is
        established.
  \item \textbf{Sharper stochastic concentration.}  Replace the present
        finite-horizon moment/union bound with a stopped-supermartingale or
        Gaussian quadratic-form concentration result on the same source funnel.
  \item \textbf{Transition-aware regularizer amplitude.}  If the full-state
        model predicts a stationary--transition conflict outside the currently
        tested transition, preserve the steady-state regularizer mapping and
        test asymmetric or rate-limited response during changing seas.
  \item \textbf{Reference-point and GNSS validation.}  Validate the lever-arm
        model at a surveyed nonzero offset and add GNSS-aided separation of slow
        absolute trajectory from bounded wave motion, including asynchronous
        timing, outages, and reacquisition.
  \item \textbf{Horizontal observability.}  Quantify how GNSS aiding, improved
        tilt information, and accelerometer-bias observability reduce the
        low-frequency horizontal acceleration-error density identified in
        Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits}.
  \item \textbf{Vessel-response realism.}  Apply hull response-amplitude
        operators to the directional sea and include off-CoG rotational
        acceleration so validation distinguishes ocean forcing from vessel
        response.
  \item \textbf{Multimodal and multidirectional seas.}  Test crossing seas,
        independent swell and wind-sea components, multiple peaks, heading
        changes, and time-varying directional spectra.
  \item \textbf{Wind-heel separation.}  Validate the companion slow-equilibrium
        heel model with apparent wind and vessel motion, including long swell,
        asymmetric loading, sensor lever-arm motion, and gust transients.
  \item \textbf{Temperature-dependent bias.}  Evaluate temperature variation,
        calibration error, thermal lag, hysteresis, and residual-bias
        correlation time against measured temperature histories.
  \item \textbf{Magnetic calibration under heading excitation.}  Use
        multi-heading data to identify soft-iron and sensor-misalignment terms
        beyond the present additive hard-iron model.
  \item \textbf{Independent physical validation and embedded margins.}  Repeat
        the paired protocol against an external displacement reference or
        commercial MRU and measure execution time, memory, power, and deadline
        margin across update rates and sensor configurations.
\end{itemize}